            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"MATCH\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A garment primarily symbolizes a person's spiritual state or standing before God, most notably righteousness, salvation, and purity. Clean or white garments represent righteousness (whether imputed by God or referring to the righteous deeds of the saints), while soiled or filthy garments represent sin, guilt, and defilement.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Garments symbolize righteousness, specifically the righteous acts of the saints, and can be soiled by blood and sin.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Righteousness, purity, and spiritual standing (with soiled garments representing sin).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Righteousness (righteous acts of the saints), with soiled garments representing sin.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"SAME_CASES\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"None. The book's focus on \\\"the righteous acts of the saints\\\" highlights a well-documented aspect of the consensus (often drawn directly from Revelation 19:8).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's definition matches the primary consensus meaning precisely. Both identify garments with righteousness and purity, noting that they can be defiled or soiled by sin, and both rely on the exact same central proof texts (Zechariah 3, Revelation 3, and Revelation 19).\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"One might argue the book narrowly restricts the meaning to 'righteous acts' (works) rather than imputed righteousness (salvation), but this is a standard intra-consensus variation rather than a material divergence from the core symbolic domain.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
